# `:platform` module

Owns all OS capabilities and platform APIs (Bluetooth, Location, Camera, Push Notifications, sensors, permissions).

## Placement

- Public API in `commonMain` (interfaces / `expect` declarations).
- Android impl in `androidMain` (AndroidX / platform SDKs).
- iOS impl in `iosMain` (Apple frameworks).
- No Compose theme or feature screens here. Platform view-interop `@Composable`s (e.g. a `WebView` / `WKWebView` wrapper) are allowed; keep chrome in `:features`.

Naming for co-located `expect`/`actual`: `Foo.kt` / `Foo.android.kt` / `Foo.ios.kt`. When a platform genuinely cannot support a capability, provide an `actual` that reports it unsupported rather than omitting the file.

> **A missing `actual` is invisible to an Android-only build** — `assembleDebug` passes green while iOS cannot link. Always compile both targets.

## Practices

- Follow official Google and Apple guidance for the capability (permissions, privacy, background limits) — see `platform-best-practices`.
- **Prefer existing KMP libraries** (Ktor for HTTP, multiplatform-settings for key-value storage, a multiplatform DB for persistence) before hand-rolling an `expect`/`actual`. Reserve `:platform` implementations for capabilities lacking a standard shared library.
- App hosts (`:androidApp` / `iosApp`) MUST declare every permission and privacy usage description the code requests.
- Prefer small, testable facades over leaking platform types into `:features`. **`:features` MUST NOT call Android/iOS SDKs directly.**
- Add tests for new platform behavior (host/unit where possible; document device-only gaps).

```text
platform/
  commonMain/.../<capability>/   # e.g. location/, camera/, bluetooth/, notifications/
  androidMain/.../<capability>/
  iosMain/.../<capability>/
```

## Anti-patterns

- ❌ `android.*` or `platform.*` imports in `commonMain`
- ❌ Declaring a permission with no `actual` that requests it, or requesting one never declared
- ❌ Adding an `expect` where a KMP library already covers the need
